# ---------------------------------------------------------------- secrets --
#
# Each service also has its own .gitignore covering these. Repeated here so the
# rule holds even if one of those is edited or moved: a secret reaching a
# pushed commit cannot be taken back by deleting it later, only by rewriting
# history AND rotating the credential.
#
# backend/.env   MongoDB Atlas URI, JWT_SECRET_KEY, ADMIN_EMAILS
# compiler/.env  the same JWT secret, GEMINI_API_KEY, the Atlas URI
.env
.env.*
!.env.sample

# ------------------------------------------------------------- dependencies --
node_modules/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ builds --
dist/
build/

# --------------------------------------------------------------- test data --
#
# 122 MB across 73 problems, and hidden test cases do not belong in a public
# repo in any case: pushing them hands every expected output to anyone who
# clones it.
#
# It does not need to be in the repo to rebuild the judge —
# scripts/import/runner.js regenerates the tree from the problem definitions.
#
# cses_problems/ is the leftover CSES source archive (4.3 GB). The 400 CSES
# problems were cut from the project, so nothing reads it any more; it is
# ignored here only so a copy still sitting on a working tree cannot be staged
# by accident.
cses_problems/
compiler/testdata/

# Scratch directories the judge writes submitted code into, then deletes.
compiler/codes/
compiler/outputs/

# --------------------------------------------------------------- notes, OS --
#
# Source material for the Core CS section rather than code: third-party PDFs
# and cheat sheets, some of them signed by their authors. Kept out of the repo
# because they are not ours to redistribute.
csnotes/

.DS_Store
*.log
*.bak
deploy/.env

# deploy/testdata/ is deliberately NOT ignored.
#
# It is 122 MB and it is the hidden test cases, so committing it is a real
# trade and not an oversight. The reason is mechanical: Render builds the judge
# image from this repository, and it has no persistent disk or volume to mount
# test data from the way the EC2 compose file does. Anything the judge needs at
# run time has to be in the repository for Render to see it at build time.
#
# What this costs: anyone who can read this repo can read every expected
# output. For 73 standard interview problems that is a much smaller secret than
# it sounds — but if the repo is public, assume the tests are public too, and do
# not treat a passing verdict here as proof somebody solved it unaided.
#
# To take it back: re-add the ignore, run `git rm -r --cached deploy/testdata`,
# and deploy to EC2 with deploy/docker-compose.prod.yml instead, which mounts
# the directory at run time and never puts it in an image or a commit.
